the iceberg known as a68a  is one of the biggest ever recorded more  than four thousand square meters  it broke away from the antarctic larsen  sea ice shelf in 2017  and is drifting towards south georgia at  about 20 kilometers a day  it's absolutely massive in terms of  icebergs mostly icebergs when they're in  the open ocean  uh they tend to break up and go into  smaller icebergs this one remarkably  has remained intact and that is still  even more remarkable  because it's got quite a shallow keel  it's not very thick so we'd expect it to  break apart  a68a is about 200 meters thick  and one fear is that the iceberg will  run aground when it reaches the  continental  shelf around the island the challenge  facing the team  is to assess the impact something so  huge might have on the vulnerable south  atlantic ecosystem  so something the size of luxembourg is  going to be a massive  impact on an island like south georgia  which isn't that much bigger than  luxembourg itself  the iceberg is formed from glacier ice  and as it melts will release billions of  tons of fresh water into the sea  potentially devastating krill  phytoplankton  and other species low on the food chain  that in turn  could threaten the diverse wildlife on  the island  it's one of the most pristine places in  the world with the ocean of fauna at  its most vulnerable that's the problem  the scientists will employ two robotic  submarines to measure the temperature  salinity and levels of phytoplankton in  the water around the iceberg  they'll also collect and study animals  in the water  if we don't protect the oceans this kind  of events will become increasingly  frequent  and the impact irreversible greenpeace  and others have long  been warning of the effects of climate  change but say the threat presented by  this enormous iceberg  is one that cannot be ignored  daniel schweiner al jazeera juan osiris
 

